{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791030571,"nonce":"Cgh9ek6jvKyfdRhLASby7oo414M=","title":"Measure CPU use and frame pacing on Linux/Wayland","message":"Wayland is where Blinc struggled before (Blinc's git-bug e17d13a: idle freeze, event loop starving the swapchain acquire; d6037ec: vkAcquireNextImage latency). Measure ashui's CPU per phase and how FIFO presentation is paced by the compositor's frame callbacks, on the NUC (Ubuntu 26.04, Intel Iris Xe).\n\nSetup:\n- tools/demo/Pacing.hx steps through idle, animate, caret, hover (synthetic pointer sweep), scroll (wheel deltas each tick) and idle-after on its own timers, printing phase boundaries.\n- tools/demo/pacing.py runs it, cuts ASHUI_FRAME_LOG by phase and samples /proc per thread, so ashui's own threads stay apart from a software renderer's.\n- Over ssh there is no GPU (raikage is not in render/video; the render node is held by the seat's session), so the run happens inside xwindow's gnome_session.py: a private headless gnome-shell with a virtual monitor, rendering through lavapipe. The compositor's frame clock and Wayland frame callbacks are real; the rasterizing CPU is not representative, which is why threads are reported apart.\n- Native libraries built on the NUC under ~/.cache/ashui-nuc; Ash from ash-fd.\n\nMac baseline (Ash hybrid, 3 s phases): animate, hover and scroll at 60 fps, frame gap p50 16.6 ms p95 under 17 ms, ashui's own work p50 0.1-0.3 ms; idle draws nothing. On macOS the frame wait shows in the window wait, not in present.","files":null}]}